[38;5;231m[49m⏺[39m [1mThe Sea Keeps Its Own Time[0m

  Before the first light, the sea
  rolls in grey and patient,
  folding itself over the stones
  the way a sleeper turns in the night.

  It does not hurry.
  It has done this since the land was young,
  and it will do this after our names
  are only letters on a stone.

  Children run from it, then back to it.
  They build castles of wet sand
  and watch the tide take them apart,
  one grain at a time, without anger.

  Fishermen read it like a page.
